To resolve Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) errors in Next.js with WordPress, you need to configure the CORS settings correctly on both your Next.js and WordPress sides.

### 1. Configure CORS on WordPress

#### Step 1: Add `wp-cors.php` File
Create or edit an empty file named `wp-cors.php` in your WordPress root directory. This file will handle the CORS headers.

```php
<?php
// Disable default header
header_remove('Access-Control-Allow-Origin');
header_remove('Access-Control-Allow-Methods');
header_remove('Access-Control-Allow-Headers');

// Allow specific origins
$allow_origins = ['https://your-nextjs-app.com'];

foreach ($allow_origins as $origin) {
    // Set Access-Control-Allow-Origin
    header("Access-Control-Allow-Origin: {$origin}");

    // Set Access-Control-Allow-Methods
    header("Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, POST, OPTIONS");

    // Set Access-Control-Allow-Headers
    header("Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Content-Type, Authorization, X-Requested-With");

    // Handle preflight requests
    if ($_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'] === 'OPTIONS') {
        exit;
    }
}

?>
```

